menuconfig MCUHOME_SENSOR
	bool "Generic Zephyr-sensor channel adapter"
	depends on SENSOR
	depends on MCUHOME_MATTER
	help
	  Periodic sampling of Zephyr sensor devices into MCUHome channels
	  (docs/design/component-model.md §5): one workqueue-driven poller
	  reads a static binding array, converts each reading into the bound
	  Matter attribute's raw unit and publishes it on change.

	  This is the "peripheral" component kind's shared runtime — a sensor
	  chip whose Zephyr driver speaks the standard sensor API needs a
	  devicetree node and a binding table row, and no C code of its own.

	  The MCUHOME_MATTER dependency is a v1 limitation, not a design
	  position: channels publish into the Matter attribute store and call
	  mcuhome_matter_attr_changed(), which only exists when the Matter
	  runtime is built. When a second northbound protocol lands (CoAP,
	  ADR 0010) this becomes a choice of publication targets.

if MCUHOME_SENSOR

config MCUHOME_SENSOR_MAX_BINDINGS
	int "Maximum number of sensor channel bindings"
	default 8
	range 1 64
	help
	  Size of the poller's static per-binding state pool (last reported
	  value, sampling countdown, fault latch). No heap after init, so
	  this is a hard bound: mcuhome_sensor_start() returns -ENOSPC for a
	  larger array rather than sampling a prefix of it.

	  One binding per sensor channel, not per sensor chip — a BMP180
	  bound to a temperature and a pressure endpoint costs two.

config MCUHOME_SENSOR_WORKQ_STACK_SIZE
	int "Sensor poller workqueue stack size"
	default 2048
	help
	  Stack for the poller thread. It has to cover the deepest Zephyr
	  sensor driver call in the node's binding array, including the
	  floating-point compensation maths several Bosch drivers do on the
	  stack. 2048 covers the drivers this framework has been run with;
	  raise it when adding a driver with a heavier fetch path.

config MCUHOME_SENSOR_WORKQ_PRIO
	int "Sensor poller workqueue thread priority"
	default 10
	help
	  Preemptible and deliberately low. Sensor sampling has no deadline
	  worth defending against the networking stack, and a fetch on a
	  slow I2C part blocks for tens of milliseconds — which is also why
	  the poller has its own queue instead of using the system
	  workqueue.

endif # MCUHOME_SENSOR
